                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"}> existential quantifier
                ).
          %p
            A
            %strong coherent formula
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formula"} formula
            in coherent logic.
          %p
            A
            %strong coherent sequent
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
            of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi φ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi \vdash \psi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varphi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            are coherent formulas, possibly with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variables"} free variables
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1,\dots,x_n
          %p In full first-order logic, such a sequent is equivalent to the single formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∀
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi φ
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\forall x_1, \dots, \forall x_n (\varphi \Rightarrow \psi)
          %p
            (in the empty
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            %em sequents
            rather than merely formulas, it can also be thought of as allowing one instance of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⇒
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
            and a string of
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
          %p
            Coherent logic (including sequents, as above) is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos"} classifying topos
            of a coherent theory is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+topos"} coherent topos
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Any (finitary)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
                is a coherent theory, as is any theory in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
                of an algebraic theory is not the same as its syntactic category when regarded as a coherent theory: the latter is the “free coherent category” generated by the former (which is a category with finite products).
            %li
              %p
                A good example of a coherent theory that is not
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic
                (in any of the usual senses, although it comes from algebra) is the theory of a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 = 1 \vdash \bot
                and
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x + y = 1 \vdash (\exists z.x \cdot z = 1) \vee (\exists z.y \cdot z = 1)
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+ordered+set"} totally ordered set
                is another example of a coherent theory that is not algebraic. One adds to the algebraic theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+semigroup"} commutative semigroup
                the totality axiom
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ,
                            %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mi y
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mi y
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash_{x,y} (x \sqcup y = x) \vee (x \sqcup y = y)
            %li
              %p
                Similar examples are the theories of a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+field"} discrete field
            %li
              %p
                The theory of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
                is coherent. The irreflexivity axiom is expressed as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo #
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \# x) \vdash \bot
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo #
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo #
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \# y) \vdash (y \# x)
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo #
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi y
                        %mo .
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo #
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo #
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \# z) \vdash \exists y.(x \# y) \vee (y \# z)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo #
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg(x\# y) \vdash (x=y)
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tight+apartness+relations"} tight apartness relations
                is not coherent since it uses negation.
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+set"} classical set
                is coherent, since the theory of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
                is coherent, and the axiom
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi x
                      %mo #
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y \vee x \# y
                is also coherent.
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} total order
                is coherent, though also not algebraic. The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash_{x,y} (x\le y) \vee (y\le x)
                is coherent but not essentially algebraic.
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+weak+order"} strict weak order
                is coherent. The irreflexivity axiom is expressed as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x\lt x)\vdash \bot
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \lt y) \wedge (y \lt x) \vdash \bot
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi y
                        %mo .
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi z
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \lt z) \vdash \exists y.(x \lt y) \vee (y \lt z)
            %li
              %p
                However, the theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-order"} pseudo-order
                is not coherent since it uses the connectedness axiom
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ¬
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg (x \lt y), \neg (y \lt x) \vdash (x=y)
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+total+order"} strict total order
                is coherent since it is a strict weak order that also satisfies
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trichotomy"} trichotomy
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊤
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∨
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi y
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top \vdash (x=y) \vee (x\lt y) \vee (y\lt x)
            %li
              %p
                Since the theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+ring"} local ring
                and a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+weak+order"} strict weak order
                is coherent, this means that the theory of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+local+ring"} ordered local ring
                is also coherent, where we have
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \lt y) \vee (y \lt x) \vdash \exists z.y \cdot z = 1 + x \cdot z
              %p and
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi z
                      %mo .
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists z.x \cdot z = 1 \vdash (0 \lt x) \vee (x \lt 0)
            %li
              %p
                The theory of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+ring"} reduced ring
                is coherent, with the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+nilradical"} trivial nilradical
                axiom being expressed as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \cdot x = 0 \vdash x = 0
            %li
              %p
                Similarly, the theory of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
                is coherent, with the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  zero-product property
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/zero-product+property"} ?
                axiom being expressed as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \cdot y = 0) \vdash (x = 0) \vee (y = 0)
            %li
              %p
                Since
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GCD+rings"} GCD rings
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9zout+rings"} Bézout rings
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theories"} algebraic theories
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domains"} integral domains
                are coherent,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GCD+domains"} GCD domains
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9zout+domains"} Bézout domains
                are coherent as well.
            %li
              %p
                The theory of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
                is coherent. However, the well-pointedness condition for a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-pointed+topos"} well-pointed topos
                is not coherent, which means that the theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
                is not coherent. See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
                for more details.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p Coherent logic has many pleasing properties.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every finitary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order theory
                is equivalent, over
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
                %em Morleyization
                and can be obtained by adding new relations representing each first-order formula and its negation, with axioms that guarantee (over classical logic) these relations are interpreted correctly (using the facts that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi P
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi P
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (P\Rightarrow Q) \dashv\vdash (\neg P \vee Q)
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo ¬
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ¬
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\forall x, P) \dashv\vdash (\neg \exists x, \neg P)
                in classical logic). See D1.5.13 in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
            %li
              %p
                By (one of the theorems called)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+completeness+theorem"} Deligne's theorem
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+topos"} coherent topos
                has
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} enough points
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                are sufficient to detect provability in coherent logic. By Morleyization, we can obtain from this the classical
                = succeed "." do
                  %span.newWikiWord
                    completeness theorem for first-order logic
                    %a{:href => "/nlab/new/completeness+theorem+for+first-order+logic"} ?
            %li
              %p
                Coherent logic also satisfies a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em definability theorem
                = succeed "-model" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
                way, then that relation is named by some coherent formula in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
            %li
              %p
                It follows that if a morphism of coherent theories (i.e. an interpretation of one coherent theory in another) induces an equivalence of their categories of models in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
                of theories (i.e. induces an equivalence of classifying toposes, hence an equivalence of categories of models in all
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conceptual+completeness"} conceptual completeness
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
                without being Morita equivalent, if the former equivalence is not induced by a morphism of theories; see for instance D3.5.4 in the Elephant.)
          %p However, here is a property which one might expect coherent theories to have, but which they do not.
          %ul
            %li
              The category of models of a coherent theory (in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+category"} accessible category
              (this is true more generally for models of any logical theory). However, it need not be
              %em finitely
              accessible (i.e.
              = succeed "-accessible)." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
              such that for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              there exists a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              with
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\prec y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N},\lt)
              of this theory does not admit any morphism from an (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              \-)compact object. (However, many coherent theories do have a finitely accessible category of
              = succeed "-models.)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
          %h2#variations Variations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Sometimes coherent logic is called
                = succeed "," do
                  %em geometric logic
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric logic
            %li
              %p
                Conversely, geometric logic is sometimes called
                %em coherent
                , e.g. in Reyes (
                %a{:href => "#Reyes"}> 1977
                ), so that coherent logic in the nLab terminology corresponds to the
                %em finitary
                fragment only.
            %li
              %p
                Occasionally the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifiers"} existential quantifiers
                in coherent logic are further restricted to range only over
                = succeed "." do
                  %em finitely presented types
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"}> finitely complete category
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+functor"} cartesian functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+logic"} cartesian logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+theory"} cartesian theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"}> regular category
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+functor"} regular functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+coverage"} regular coverage
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+topos"} regular topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"}> coherent category
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+functor"} coherent functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong coherent logic
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+theory"} coherent theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+coverage"} coherent coverage
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+topos"} coherent topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+category"}> geometric category
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+functor"} geometric functor
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+locale"} coherent locale
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+mathematics"} coherent mathematics
          %h2#references References
          %p An early reference is
          %ul
            %li
              M.-F. Coste,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michel+Coste"} M. Coste
              %em Théories cohérentes et topos cohérents
              , Séminaire de théorie des catégories dirigé par Jean Bénabou Mai 1975. (
              %a{:href => "https://perso.univ-rennes1.fr/michel.coste/publis/coherent.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p A survey of results on geometric and coherent logic is in
          %ul
            %li#Reyes77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"}> Gonzalo Reyes
              ,
              %em Sheaves and concepts: A model-theoretic interpretation of Grothendieck topoi
              , Cah. Top. Diff. Géo. Cat.
              %strong XVIII
              no.2 (1977) pp.405-437. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1977__18_2_105_0"}> numdam
              )
          %p A standard textbook account of coherent logic (called ‘geometric logic’ there) can be found in
          %ul
            %li#MM94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+Mac+Lane"}> Saunders Mac Lane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"} Ieke Moerdijk
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sheaves+in+Geometry+and+Logic"} Sheaves in Geometry and Logic
              , Springer Heidelberg 1994.
          %p Properties of the generic model of a coherent theory are investigated in
          %ul
            %li
              Marc Bezem,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrik+Buchholtz"} Ulrik Buchholtz
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
              %em Syntactic Forcing Models for Coherent Logic
              , arXiv:72.07743 (2017). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.07743"}> abstract
              )
          %p Else consider the monographs
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                = succeed "," do
                  %em First Order Categorical Logic: Model-theoretical methods in the theory of topoi and related categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"} Robert Paré
                %em Accessible categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
                vol 2. Part D
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Adamek"} Jiri Adamek
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"} Jiri Rosicky
                %em Locally presentable and accessible categories
